>I am certainly not too good for NetHack. I haven't even ascended. ;)
But even so, I think my lack of ascension at this point stems from a
certain amount of carelessness on my part rather than the difficulty of

the game in general. <

That is, IMO, the first and last rule of nethack.   Most of my deaths
have been due to carelessness, and I have ascended 3 times
competitively, not counting wizard or exploration mode or savescumming.


>but my point is, once you're pretty thoroughly educated (spoiled) in
how the game works, you realize that the pieces are all there. <

I respectfully disagree.

I was throughly spoiled several years before I ascended a character.
Part of that is lack of time (I have a real life, and nethack is a
sometime thing for me -- there are many other games) but a lot of it is
not knowing how to use the knowledge.    Knowledge is NOT the same
thing as experience.

The RNG giveth, and the RNG taketh away. Yes, the RNG can sometimes be
downright generous with the goodies.  It can also be downright generous
with a pack of orcs or soldier ants when you're least prepared for
them.   It can also be downright generous by giving a Gnome a wand of
death when I don't have reflection. Or you can be a stung by a killer
bee and die instantly from the poison.  Sometimes I don't even see it
coming - just a disintegration blast from out of my vision.

In the last three games I got some really wonderful goodies within the
first three levels of the mines.  All three characters died even before
reaching the oracle.

And that's because Nethack ISN'T about items.  The most useful tool I
have is the one between my real-life ears.  A poor player can be given
all the goodies in the world and will still die.   A good player knows
how to keep a weak character alive long enough to scrounge what he
needs.  For proof, I point you to any number of newbie games played in
explore mode where they START with a wand of wishing.

>You have a very very good chance of finding any or all off the
following: FooDSM, co-aligned altar, boots of various flavors, helm of
brilliance, gauntlets of power<

Two things:

1. You have a good chance of finding one or two of them. But if you
want to ascend, you need EVERYTHING in the ascension kit. Finding all
the necessary pieces can take a long time, and if you're missing a
critical piece (I didn't find an amulet of reflection in some games
until I wished for it at the castle) life can be lethal, as a death ray
or a polymorph trap can undo all your hard work in an instant.

2.  I don't think the RNG is over-generous with the goodies, because if
there's three things I've learned about ascension they are 1) to be
prepared for any contingency 2) to carry a spare of everything and 3)
don't take stupid risks.     When you look at the necessity to
over-prepare I think you'll see that the apparent generosity of the RNG
is, in fact, a paucity.

"wishing on a lamp, a smoky
potion, a throne, or a fountain. "

Wishing on a lamp = potentially hostile genie,  death.
smoky potion = ditto.
throne = curses, hits to stats,  monster summoning, blinding compared
to a VERY small chance of a wish.
fountain = nymph, snakes, water demon, small chance of wish.

Of the methods you name all but the wand carry some degree of risk, and
two are VERY risky. In the last three years I have got perhaps ONE wish
out of a throne EVER.

" I just feel like we're guaranteed all this *stuff* and
so NetHack becomes less about ingenuity and more about applying
guaranteed formulas for success. "

Uhmmm ... don't you think that this talk about "guaranteed formula for
success" would have more weight if you could ascend regularly :)?

I HAVE ascended, and I say there's no such thing as a "guaranteed
formula for success".  There are things that work most of the time but
there's always a flaw in the ointment.  Ingenuity and cunning are
indispensable in any Nethack game.  I've found bones piles where
characters had died with dragon mail armor and amulets of reflection
and Greyswandir AND Mjollnir and they still died because they thought
their items made them invincible.  They were mistaken.

In every game I've ever ascended in, I've had one major break and at
least one major catastrophe.  EVERY game.  Being trapped.  Being
punished.  Losing my armor to a polytrap.  Turned into a killer bee.
Being seduced by a nymph.  Throwing my artifact into a lake. Having
Rodney steal the amulet and chase him halfway across Gehennom.   And so
on.

It's the ability of a player to make the most of the lucky breaks and
survive the disasters that makes the difference between ascending and
dying.

I say Nethack is hard enough as it is.  It's especially hard enough for
new players. If the dev team does decide to make the game harder, I
hope they will put a difficulty switch in. The game is hard enough for
newbies. 

Respectfully, 

